{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1780834294348,"sessionID":"ses_15e03e9d8ffeBBPee3op0wEy66","part":{"id":"prt_ea1feda49001HHQr1tpgYJHBW1","messageID":"msg_ea1fe8432001y6uOuNj5vfZ61o","sessionID":"ses_15e03e9d8ffeBBPee3op0wEy66","snapshot":"d2d5146b8e1fd75a1d2d989663d2df6a9df95032","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780834294378,"sessionID":"ses_15e03e9d8ffeBBPee3op0wEy66","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_x87d11eu","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"---\n# Proposed System for Dependency and Tool Updates\n\n## Strategy: \"The Continuous Update Loop\"\n\nSince the infrastructure is fully automated (Terraform + Ansible), we should embrace a model where the single source of truth—the repository—is kept up-to/date. Instead of trying to manage updates *on* the running VM, we update the *definitions* in this repo and re-run the provisioning playbook.\n\n### 1. Decoupling Versions from Logic\nWe will move all hardcoded versions out of `provision-opencode.yml` and into a structured configuration file (e.g., `ansible/group_vars/opencode_vms/versions.yml`). This allows us to see exactly what is currently deployed.\n\n### 2. The Update Workflow\n1.  **Detection**: A scheduled CI job (in Gitea Actions or similar) runs a \"Check for Updates\" script.\n    *   For `npm` packages (`opencode`, `openchamber`), it checks the registry/GitHub releases.\n    *   For binaries (`gh`, `tea`, `rtk`), it checks GitHub release tags via API.\n2.  **Proposal**: If an update is found, the CI job automatically creates a new branch and a Pull Request (PR) updating the version string in `versions.yml`. The PR description includes: \"Update [tool] from vX to vY\".\n3.  **Verification (Optional but Recommended)**: Since we can rebuild, if tests pass on the new version, it's safe. Given this is a personal lab, the updated PR itself is the verification trigger.\n4.  **Deployment**: Merging the PR triggers the Ansible playbook to run against the `opencode_vms`. Because the playbook is idempotent and uses the new versions specified in `versions.yml`, the VM is \"updated\" by being re-provisioned or simply having its binaries replaced/reinstalled as per the logic.\n\n### 3. Handling \"Broken\" Versions (The Safety Net)\nBecause we log every version change in Git, we have a complete history of what worked. If `v2.94.0` of `gh` breaks the provisioning:\n1.  Identify the failure in CI logs.\n2.  Revert the commit in the repository.\n3.  Run Ansible. The VM is restored to the previous known-good state (`v2.93.0`).\n\n### 4. Implementation Plan (Proceeding Now)\nI will implement the following changes:\n1.  Create `ansible/group_vars/opencode_vms/versions.yml` containing all current versions extracted from the playbook.\n2.  Refactor `ansible/playbooks/provision-opencode.yml` to use these variables instead of hardcoded strings.\n3.  (Planned) Create a shell script `scripts/check-tool-updates.sh` that can be used in CI to detect new versions for the tools currently managed.\n\n---\n","filePath":"docs/automation/update_strategy.md"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/ubuntu/.local/state/cpe/clones/01KTGS5J7NTEVBV19WZS2JHXBH/docs/automation/update_strategy.md","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"docs/automation/update_strategy.md","time":{"start":1780834294356,"end":1780834294374}},"id":"prt_ea1feda4c001mgfUM8aXqIoVvv","sessionID":"ses_15e03e9d8ffeBBPee3op0wEy66","messageID":"msg_ea1fe8432001y6uOuNj5vfZ61o"}}
